result:
This handler has a critical SQL injection vulnerability, plus a few secondary issues:

## Critical: SQL Injection

```js
// Vulnerable — attacker controls req.params.id
const query = `SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ${req.params.id}`;
```

An attacker can send `GET /api/users/1 OR 1=1` to dump the entire users table, or `1; DROP TABLE users--` to execute arbitrary SQL. Fix with a parameterized query:

```js
app.get('/api/users/:id', async (req, res) => {
  const { id } = req.params;

  // Validate input shape before hitting the DB
  if (!/^\d+$/.test(id)) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Invalid user ID' });
  }

  try {
    const [user] = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [id]);
    if (!user) return res.status(404).json({ error: 'Not found' });
    res.json(user);
  } catch (err) {
    res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal server error' });
  }
});
```

## Secondary Issues

| Issue | Risk | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `SELECT *` | Leaks sensitive columns (passwords, tokens, PII) | Select only the columns the client needs |
| No auth/authz check | Any user can fetch any other user's data (IDOR) | Verify `req.user.id === id` or check a permission |
| Unhandled rejection | Crashes the process on DB error, no response sent to client | Wrap in `try/catch` or add `.catch()` |
| No input validation | Non-integer IDs cause unexpected DB behavior | Validate before querying (shown above) |

The SQL injection is the blocker — parameterize that query before anything else ships.
